    "case_summary": "A 42-year-old woman experienced an intermittent dry cough for ten months. She did not report fever, chest tightness, bone pain, anorexia, dyspnea on exertion, or weight loss. She took medication from a local clinic to manage her cough, but began to produce blood-tinged sputum one month before hospital admission. Physical examination revealed no bone pain or lymphadenopathy. Auscultation indicated slightly coarse breathing without rales or rhonchi. Imaging studies, including a chest X-ray and computed tomography (CT), showed a right anterior mediastinal shadow and multiple pulmonary nodular lesions. The patient had no history of smoking, occupational or environmental exposure to air pollutants or microorganisms, and no family history of similar symptoms or cancer. She had not traveled recently. Bronchoscopic examination found no endobronchial lesions or abnormal secretions. Pulmonary function tests indicated a mild restrictive ventilatory defect. A whole-body bone scan showed no tumor involvement. Hemogram, leukocyte differentiation count, and coagulating profile were all within normal limits.\n\nThe microscopic examination showed a solid mass made up mostly of plasma cells. These tumor cells stained positively for kappa light chains, but negatively for lambda chains.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
